.TH FUZZYGENE 1 2000-1月 "fuzzygene_context_upstream" "fuzzygene_context_upstream"
.SH NAME
fuzzygene_context_upstream \- Inject the result of an **upstream perturbation** as the control baseline (iterative perturbation).
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIfuzzygene_context_upstream(\fBfuzzygene\fR as <generic> FuzzyGeneModel, 
\fBupstream\fR as <generic> InterventionResult, 
\fBuseMutant\fR as boolean = TRUE, 
\fBminCoverage\fR as double = 0, 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Inject the result of an **upstream perturbation** as the control baseline (iterative perturbation).
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBfuzzygene\fB \fR\- a trained model. 
.PP
.PP
\fBupstream\fB \fR\- an `InterventionResult`, e.g. one element of `knockouts(...)`. 
.PP
.PP
\fBuseMutant\fB \fR\- take the mutant (perturbed) state instead of the wildtype one. Default TRUE.. 
.PP
.PP
\fBminCoverage\fB \fR\- minimum fraction (0-1) of model genes the upstream result must cover; 0 disables the check. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment used to report errors. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
a multi line coverage summary, or an R# error object when the context is rejected
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
After this call the mutant state of the upstream experiment becomes the reference state of the
 model, so the next perturbation reports "effect of B given that A is already knocked out"
 instead of "effect of B from the original baseline".
 
\fB
 let round1 = model |> knockouts(c("A"));           # A 失活
 print(fuzzygene::fuzzygene_context_upstream(model, round1[[1]]));
 let round2 = model |> knockouts(c("B"));           # 在 A 失活的背景上敲 B
 
\fR`

 


 


 \fBuseMutant = TRUE\fR (default) takes \fBMutantMeans\fR; \fBFALSE\fR takes \fBWildtypeMeans\fR, which
 restores the reference frame of the upstream round. An upstream result flagged as
 \fBUndefined\fR (the target gene was not modelled) is rejected instead of being silently applied.
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
FuzzyGene
